      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The math does use a clever trick to preserve the signal's original structure. It assumes that the signal is periodic, meaning it has a repeating pattern of waves. The Fourier transform takes advantage of this by performing a discrete Fourier series (DFS) decomposition, where the signal is broken down into discrete frequency components, typically at integer frequencies. The DFS decomposes the signal into its constituent sine waves, preserving their phase relationships and amplitude ratios within each frequency component, allowing them to \"stay apart\" from one another.",
